  "title": "Choose the tense and tell it",
  "lesson": {
    "kind": "deepen",
    "focusPoint": 2,
    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Point 1: Review of the last class",
        "body": "What did you learn in the last class?\n\nLast class you studied the simple past, the continuous and the perfect. Today you pick the tense with clue words."
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "heading": "Point 2: Clue words",
        "body": "Yesterday you rode the Mérida cable car. Compare “Yesterday I sang” and “I sing every day”. What tells you the tense?\n\nYesterday and last week: simple past. Every day and now: simple present. Clue words show you which station."
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "heading": "Point 3: Clues for the future",
        "body": "Tomorrow you ride the Mérida cable car again. Which clue word and tense?\n\nTomorrow and next week: simple future. “I will ride the cable car tomorrow.” Will goes before the verb."
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "heading": "Point 4: Long and short actions",
        "body": "You were riding when your phone rang. Which action is long? Which is short?\n\nWas riding is the long action. Rang is the sudden one: simple past. “I was riding when the phone rang.”"
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "heading": "Point 5: Already and just",
        "body": "Compare “I finished yesterday” and “I have already finished”. What changed?\n\nAlready and just: present perfect. The trip is done, and you are here now. “I have already finished the trip.”"
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "heading": "Point 6: Pick and tell",
        "body": "Be a guide of the Mérida cable car. How do you pick and tell each tense?\n\nAsk: When? Is it going on? Is it done? Choose the tense, then say the clue word and a sentence, like a good guide."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "Look at the clue word and ask: When? Is it going on? Is it done? Then pick the tense.",
    "memoryPhrase": "Ask: When? Is it going on? Is it done?",
    "weekRecap": "One word: canto / cantaba / canté / cantaré / cantaría. Two words: helper haber + participle (he cantado). Learn to narrate WHEN an action happens: one-word forms and two-word forms, choosing with meaning.",
    "priorDayRecap": "Deepen: one-word times. We stayed on one-word times only (no haber): verb = action; conjugate = change for who and when; stem and ending build each form.",
